Workers no longer wait for each other: slots instead of groups

The pump started a group of jobs and awaited the whole group, so three finished workers sat
idle while the fourth kept running, and then all of them started their next step at the same
instant - which is also the worst possible moment to hit the disk. Extraction being fast now
makes that waste obvious: extraction and syncing are short, independent steps, so a worker must
be able to move on alone.

A job now occupies a slot. The dispatch decision (SubSyncService.PlanStart) starts as many jobs
as there are free slots, and a job that finishes wakes the pump immediately to refill its slot
instead of waiting for its neighbours. Consequences:

- no group barrier: one slow file no longer holds three idle workers;
- the batch spreads out over time instead of moving in lockstep, so four extractions stop
  starting in the same instant. A cold start still fills every slot at once, so starts in the
  same dispatch are staggered by 400 ms;
- a job for a media file that is already being read still waits for it (a second worker would
  race to build the same stored audio analysis); it just does not hold anyone else back;
- disks are still preferred, never required: a job is dispatched onto an idle volume first, but
  a single-disk library still runs at the full worker count.

Harness: 98 checks, ALL PASS, including "empty pool fills every slot", "one free slot starts
exactly one job", "a full pool starts nothing", "a file being read is not started twice" and
"the idle disk is taken first".

Version 1.1.0.27.
